shadowfetch-fireproof (5.0.1-1) umbra; urgency=medium

  * `fireproof update` can upgrade Fireproof itself. dpkg runs as
    fireproofd's child, inside fireproofd.service's cgroup, and every
    package since 2.1.4 shipped a preinst (dh_installsystemd --no-start)
    that stopped fireproofd on upgrade: systemd signalled the whole cgroup,
    dpkg was killed mid-upgrade, and needrestart then restarted fireproofd
    from inside its own transaction and hung until the one-hour stop
    timeout, holding the dpkg lock and blocking reboot.
    - debian/rules: fireproofd.service gets --no-stop-on-upgrade; no
      maintainer script stops, starts or restarts it any more.
    - fireproofd.service: KillMode=mixed. A stop sends SIGTERM to the daemon
      only, which already defers it until dpkg has finished; dpkg, apt and
      maintainer scripts are no longer signalled with it.
    - New conffile /etc/needrestart/conf.d/50-shadowfetch-fireproof.conf:
      needrestart never restarts fireproofd.
    - The new daemon still takes over after the transaction: the postinst
      restarts an idle fireproofd without waiting, or, when fireproofd is
      the one running the upgrade, queues the restart in a transient unit
      outside its cgroup that waits for the update to finish
      (/usr/libexec/fireproof-restart-after-upgrade). fireproofd also exits
      after every successful commit, so the next D-Bus activation runs the
      code and libraries now on disk.
  * Analyze refuses to offer an update on a system an earlier dpkg run left
    half-configured (packages unpacked but not configured, reinstall
    required, or anything `dpkg --audit` reports). `fireproof check` and
    `fireproof update` print the packages and the repair command,
    `sudo dpkg --configure -a && sudo apt -f install`, and exit 3; the page
    shows the same and keeps Update disabled; the commit refuses it under
    the lock as well.
  * The verify battery's "Mirror still resolves" check looks up the mirror's
    host name, not host:port: a mirror with a port was reported as not
    resolving.
  * An update never removes a package you did not ask to remove. Fireproof
    planned with python3-apt's dist-upgrade (libapt's classic resolver,
    not apt 3's solver), and during a Debian testing library transition
    (libavcodec/mlt, QA 5.0.1) it planned 14-16 removals on a 4.1-origin
    system, including shadowfetch-desktop, shadowfetch-creative-base,
    krita and kdenlive, where `apt full-upgrade` held 12 packages back and
    removed nothing.
    - fireproofd plan_update(), used by analyze and by the commit under the
      lock: when the full resolution removes a package, those packages are
      kept and protected and libapt's ResolveByKeep holds back only the
      upgrades that needed them gone; new packages that only those
      upgrades wanted (libapt garbage) are left out too; if that does not
      settle, the safe upgrade (no removals) is used. The commit refuses a
      plan that still removes such a package.
    - The only removals an update makes are retirements: required by an
      incoming shadowfetch-* package's own Conflicts/Breaks, of a package
      installed automatically. shadowfetch-desktop,
      shadowfetch-creative-base and manually installed packages are never
      removed.
    - The analysis carries `held_back` (packages, the removals avoided, and
      the message). `fireproof check`/`update` and the Fireproof page say
      which updates are held back and why: a Debian transition in progress;
      they update once it completes. Held-back updates do not count on the
      update badge.
    - A package being removed is shown as "remove <version>" in the red
      list, not as an upgrade.

 -- Shadowfetch Project <signing@shadowfetch.com>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 06:20:00 -0400

