shadowfetch-fireproof (4.1.0-1) umbra; urgency=medium

  * BREAKING: fireproofd's Verify() requires authorization. It was exported
    with neither sender_keyword nor an auth check, while running the dpkg
    audit and an apt dependency simulation as root and returning a detailed
    report of the machine's package state, and the bus policy allowed
    context="default" to send to the destination -- so any local process
    could drive that root work in a loop and read the result. It now takes
    the caller's bus name, goes through the authorization check, and holds a
    non-blocking lock so a second battery is refused with Busy.
  * BREAKING: the fireproofd bus policy is default-deny plus an explicit
    per-member allowlist. A method added without an auth check is now
    unreachable until someone lists it deliberately.
  * Add Inspect(), a read-only companion that returns the last recorded
    battery result and executes nothing. It carries no authorization
    requirement, preserving the unprivileged read path Verify was providing
    by accident, without the root execution.
  * Fireproof is the single update authority. The shared sfupdate modules --
    snapshots, state, trusted executables and the update vocabulary -- ship
    here, and shadowfetch-bundle-install stops deciding its own Phoenix
    Point. Measured on the shipped code with a previous Fireproof update
    recorded at Point 41, a bundle install printed SF-POINT 43 while
    fireproof-state.json was byte-for-byte unchanged, so the one rollback
    button still answered 41: two programs, two different answers, differing
    by two Points.
  * Ship /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/85fireproof as the only APT::Periodic
    declaration in the product. Package lists refresh daily so the badge and
    the analyze screen stay honest; nothing is downloaded or installed
    unattended.
  * Bound the work an unauthenticated Analyze() can start. It is
    unauthenticated on purpose -- the tray badge needs the update count
    without a password -- but it spawned a new apt-cache-opening thread
    inside a root daemon on every call, so any local process could start an
    unbounded number of them. A recent result is served from cache and
    callers arriving mid-run coalesce onto it: worst case is one analysis
    per 30 seconds regardless of caller count.
  * The Fireproof window is mounted inside the Control Center, so its error
    red is the Control Center's. It was a paler, oranger red, so one failure
    message changed hue depending on which frame you were looking at.

 -- Shadowfetch Project <signing@shadowfetch.com>  Wed, 09 Sep 2026 18:28:58 -0400

